# ZapBot example: a protected (encrypted) tag for what is private, with shortcuts to use it in one word.
# @manual
#
#   /stash.add <text>     saves the text in the protected tag #stash (the tag has to be unlocked)
#   /stash.view           asks the password, unlocks #stash and shows its notes
#   /stash.view <pass>    the same, with the password in the command (less safe, see below)
#   /stash.lock           locks #stash right now
#
# THE IDEA. #stash is a protected tag (see /notes -lock, docs/commands/notes.md): the text of its notes and its files
# are encrypted with a key made from a password that the bot never stores. You unlock it with /stash.view, use it, and it
# locks again by itself after notes.lockMinutes (5 by default) without use, when the bot restarts, or with /stash.lock.
#
# A DAY WITH IT
#   /stash.view                          → 🔑 Type your password:   (you type it; the bot deletes your message)
#                                          🔓 The tag #stash is unlocked, it will be locked in 5m without use.
#                                          (and the notes, shown)
#   /stash.add the bank PIN is 1234      → saved encrypted; your message leaves the history
#   /stash.lock                          → 🔒 The tag #stash is locked.
#
# PRIVACY, IN SHORT
#   - Database or backup leaked: the notes and the files are encrypted; only the password opens them.
#   - Phone lent, WhatsApp Web open on another screen, screen shared: lock it before (/stash.lock); locked, only a 🔒 shows.
#   - It only unlocks and opens in your own private chat (the messages with yourself): in a group or in someone's chat
#     it stays locked, adding and editing are refused, and it is never sent with -to.
#   - The password is never saved: not in the database, not in the logs, not in the history. Forgot it? The notes are
#     lost (there is no recovery).
#   - /stash.view <pass> puts the password inside a normal command: the bot deletes the message and hides it in the logs,
#     in the alias echo and in the history, but a phone notification can show it before it is deleted. The question
#     (/stash.view alone) is safer.
#
# PRIVATE ALIASES. The three shortcuts are created with -q -p: -p (-private) makes an alias the owner's. Nobody else
# sees them (not in /alias, not in /help, not even the extra admins) and for anyone else they do not exist. Drop the -p
# to let the users who can run the target see them in their /help (here it makes no sense: the tag only opens for you).
#
# FIRST TIME (once): protect the tag by hand. /notes -lock #stash asks a NEW password twice and encrypts the notes the tag
# already has. The shortcuts do not create the protection (a typo in the tag would ask for a password out of nowhere).
# By hand: /alias -examples secret-notes shows the aliases it creates (nothing is loaded);
# /alias -yes -examples secret-notes loads them.

/alias -q -p /stash.add -desc "Saves a note in the protected tag #stash, encrypted. The tag has to be unlocked first (/stash.view); locked, or outside your own private chat (a group, someone's chat), it refuses and the command stays out of the history" \
    /notes #stash $@

/alias -q -p /stash.lock -desc "Locks the protected tag #stash right now: afterwards only a lock shows. Use it before lending the phone or sharing the screen (it also locks by itself after notes.lockMinutes without use and when the bot restarts)" \
    /notes -lock #stash -now

/alias -q -p /stash.view -desc "Unlocks the protected tag #stash and shows its notes: asks the password in your private chat (or /stash.view password, less safe: it travels in a normal command, the bot deletes it and hides it from logs and history). Never opens in a group or with -to; locks again after notes.lockMinutes without use" \
    /notes -v -lock $1 #stash
